WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.068
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Adam Scott.

00:00:03.068 --> 00:00:05.504
So, "Severance," which, I mean,
we've been singing the praises

00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:06.873
of "Severance" on this show
for a while.

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.308
-Really nice. I know you have.
It's really cool.

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:12.011
-But this is a very high-concept
show about people who can have

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:13.980
a chip put in their brains
that will basically

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.983
separate their work persona
with their home persona.

00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:19.084
-That's right.
It separates your memory.

00:00:19.084 --> 00:00:21.187
So when you're at work,
you have no idea who you are

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:25.091
in the outside world, and then
when you cross the threshold

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:29.729
of work to go home, the chip
is deactivated so you have

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:32.498
no memory of who you are at work
or anything you do there,

00:00:32.498 --> 00:00:35.901
so you're leading
two completely separate lives.

00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:40.540
-So it's this very high-concept
sort of sci-fi idea.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.641
It is very stylized
in the shooting.

00:00:42.641 --> 00:00:46.412
It's a fantastic cast, but I
imagine in the making of it,

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:49.281
are you constantly wondering,
"Are people going to get this?"

00:00:49.281 --> 00:00:52.752
-Oh, yeah. I mean, the whole
time we were making it,

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:55.788
we were like,
"This is so weird."

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:57.590
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.659
-No clue whatsoever if anyone --

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:02.561
Like, what are people going to
do with this?

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:06.732
You know, and actually when it
started -- Like in January when

00:01:06.732 --> 00:01:08.668
the billboards went up in town,

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:12.105
the billboards were just,
like, my head, right?

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:14.607
And I started freaking out.

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.009
February 18th,
when the show was coming out,

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:19.845
it was like this locomotive
headed towards me.

00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:23.282
And I started freaking out
and calling Ben Stiller,

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:25.518
who's the executive producer,
directed a bunch of it,

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:29.521
just with these neurotic,
nitpicky calls like,

00:01:29.521 --> 00:01:31.758
"I don't know if the thing
in episode three is

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:34.694
properly setting up the thing
in episode five.

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.062
What do you think?"

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:38.331
He's like,
"Yeah, no, everything is okay."

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:39.532
And it's like, okay, alright.

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:41.700
And then I would have
some other reason.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.337
Eventually after a few of
these calls, I was just like,

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:47.707
"Ben, I think what it comes down
to is I just don't want people

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:49.942
to think I suck."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.942 --> 00:01:52.412
And he's like, "Okay, alright.

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:55.414
I don't think people are
going to think you suck."

00:01:55.414 --> 00:01:58.384
But he'd kind of been through
the thing of his head

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:00.720
being up on the side
of a building before,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.390
so he was like, "Anytime
you're freaking out, call me.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:05.558
It's all good."

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:07.360
And he was like really great
with all that.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.262
-He did an incredible job.
It's beautifully written.

00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:12.565
You know, again, it's so
stylized, and it takes place --

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:14.267
Most of it takes place
in this very sort of

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.836
sterile office environment.

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:19.005
It seems incredibly
claustrophobic.

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:21.474
Was it claustrophobic
in real life as well?

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:25.478
-Yeah, the set is strange 'cause
the carpet in our office is

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:28.814
bright green, the ceiling is
quite low.

00:02:28.814 --> 00:02:32.385
So, yeah, after nine months of
shooting in this environment,

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:34.620
we all were starting to go
a little crazy.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.325
-And on your computer screens,
the sort of menial work you do

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:41.727
is just clicking on numbers
as they start to,

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:45.431
I guess, throb would maybe be --
-Sure?

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:47.466
-That's, like -- That's my
kind of go-to word.

00:02:47.466 --> 00:02:50.002
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:50.002 --> 00:02:52.605
Like, when all else fails,
just go to "throb"?

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:53.873
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:56.842
-Alright. Just don't do that
while texting.

00:02:56.842 --> 00:02:59.379
-Obviously!
[ Laughter ]

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:01.347
-Teenagers.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:02.948
-But they ask for my number!

00:03:02.948 --> 00:03:06.185
-I get it.

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.321
-But what are you actually
doing on the screen?

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:12.792
-We are actually going around
and eliminating numbers, like,

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:13.959
exactly like what we're doing.

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:16.628
They designed
the exact computer program

00:03:16.628 --> 00:03:18.030
that you see on the show.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.400
-Because, you know, you
mentioned "The Office" earlier.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.101
On that show, people were always

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:24.337
doing different things
on their computer.

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.439
But this was you actually had to
do the number game?

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:29.008
-Yeah. On "Parks &amp; Rec,"
I remember they had Internet

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:31.110
access on all those computers
that people were at

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.245
in the background,
so you could just, like,

00:03:33.245 --> 00:03:35.181
browse the Internet on camera.

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:39.953
But on this, we were actually
eliminating numbers.

00:03:39.953 --> 00:03:44.556
Like Zach Cherry, who's in the
show, got really good at it.

00:03:44.556 --> 00:03:46.559
[ Laughter ]
I was not as good.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:49.127
And then the trackball they
designed for the specific

00:03:49.127 --> 00:03:52.332
computer is ergonomically
impossible, so we all started

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:54.634
getting this weird
new carpal tunnel.

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:56.402
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:59.905
-You're really giving a ringing
endorsement of show business.

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:01.941
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:03.342
-This was near the end
of the season.

00:04:03.342 --> 00:04:05.812
There was a dance party.
Here you are.

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:08.948
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:10.583
Here are two of
your cast members.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:14.787
This was sort of a very,
I feel like, if you can say

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:16.589
there's an iconic scene
in a new show,

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.524
but this was very much that.

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:19.825
What did you know
about the scene

00:04:19.825 --> 00:04:21.227
before you started shooting it?

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:23.829
-Well, we knew
just what was scripted.

00:04:23.829 --> 00:04:27.533
It ended up taking about two
days to shoot that whole scene,

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:31.304
and the thing that Ben had kept
from us and surprised us with

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:34.740
on the day was that the
fluorescent lights we had been

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.109
living with for months
and months,

00:04:37.109 --> 00:04:39.145
once the music starts,
Tramell Tillman,

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:42.448
who plays Milchick,
hits a remote control

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.016
and they turn into like
disco lights

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:45.751
and the entire ceiling just
starts --

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:47.953
and we had no idea
that was going to happen.

00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:51.523
So the reaction you see of us
kind of marveling

00:04:51.523 --> 00:04:55.061
at these lights are actual
actors that have been trapped

00:04:55.061 --> 00:05:00.065
in this room for nine months
being dazzled by disco lights.

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:03.269
-You do seem absolutely in awe.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:05.605
-This is such a technically
precise show.

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:10.042
I feel like you watch it and it
seems as though the actors

00:05:10.042 --> 00:05:11.710
have this incredible freedom
because you're all so good,

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:15.314
but I imagine there is something
a little bit more rigid

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:17.082
when you have to be in a show
where -- I mean, everything is

00:05:17.082 --> 00:05:19.118
so beautifully framed,
so perfectly shot.

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:23.856
-Yeah. Kind of the second
I walked onto the set I thought,

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:26.959
oh, okay, I've never really done
something like this before.

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:31.497
This is going to be all about
technical precision but also

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:35.368
being able to figure out
the acting in and out and around

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:38.170
that technical precision, which
I had never really done before.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:42.541
So I just -- Knowing that I
trusted Ben as much as I do,

00:05:42.541 --> 00:05:47.046
I just decided to give myself
over to the technical needs of

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:50.215
the show and to completely lean
on him

00:05:50.215 --> 00:05:53.986
for all of the acting stuff
because I wasn't going to be

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.388
able to keep an eye on it myself

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:58.290
'cause I had too much to do,
I guess.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:03.429
-It looks like whatever you guys
had to put into it, it paid off.

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:04.830
It couldn't have paid off better
in the show itself.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.331
-Oh, thanks.
-It's wonderful.

00:06:06.331 --> 00:06:08.167
I can't wait for more. Thank you
so much for being here.

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:09.835
-Thanks, Seth.
-Adam Scott, everybody.

00:06:09.835 --> 00:06:12.038
"Severance" is streaming on
Apple TV+.

